@article{mccullochlogical,
  title = {A {{LOGICAL CALCULUS OF THE IDEAS IMMANENT IN NERVOUS ACTIVITY}}},
  author = {Mcculloch, Warren S and Pitts, Walter},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\section{Annotations\\
(5/6/2026, 4:26:22 PM)}

\par
``.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 99)
\par
``Because of the ``all-or-none'' character of nervous activity, neural events and the relations among them can be treated by means of propositional logic.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 99) This feels like the core insight that allowed Von Neumann to look at nervous activity as a basis for computing.
\par
``The ``all-or-none'' law of nervous activity is sufficient to insure that the activity of any neuron may be represented as a proposition.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 100) The "all-or-none" law of nervous activity states that all neurons are either excited or not.
\par
``Two difficulties appeared. The first concerns facilitation and extinction, in which antecedent activity temporarily alters responsiveness to subsequent stimulation of one and the same part of the net. The second concerns learning, in which activities concurrent at some previous time have altered the net pe\textasciitilde anently, so that a stimulus which would previously have been inadequate is now adequate.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 101) Inhibition and learning both have major impacts on neural nets and ultimately transformer architecture, and feel like some of the missing bits in the original capabilities of the Von Neumann machines.
\par
``The nervous system contains many circular paths, whose activity so regenerates the excitation of any participant neuron that reference to time past becomes indefinite, although it still implies that afferent activity has realized one of a certain class of configurations over time. Precise specification of these implications by means of recursive functions, and determination of those that can be embodied in the activity of nervous nets, completes the theory.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 101) The ability to retain sensory information is not time stamped and can be described with recursive functions! This basic idea seems to be the one that sparked the memory circuits in the Von Neumann machine.
\par
``To present the theory, the most appropriate symbolism is that of Language II of Carnap (1938), augmented with various notations drawn from Russell and Whitehead (1927), including the Principia conventions for dots.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 102) It is interesting to see how far reaching the Prinicipia is and its impact on early computing and neuroscience.
\par
``Thus our knowledge of the world, including ourselves, is incomplete as to space and indefinite as to time. This ignorance, implicit in all our brains, is the counterpart of the abstraction which renders our knowledge useful. The role of brains in determining the epistemic relations of our theories to our observations and of these to the facts is all too clear, for it is apparent that every idea and every sensation is realized by activity within that net, and by no such activity are the actual afferents fully determined.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 113) In order to think, we must accept a lossy memory?
\par
``hus empiry confirms that if our nets are undefined, our facts are undefined, and to the ``real'' we can attribute not so much as one quality or ``form.'' With determination of the net, the unkowable object of knowledge, the ``thing in itself,'' ceases to be unknowable.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 113) This is maybe the fullest refutation of Kant that I have ever read.
\par
``The ``all-or-none'' law of these activities, and the conformity of their relations to those of the logic of propositions, insure that the relations of psychons are those of the twovalued logic of propositions. Thus in psychology, introspective, behavioristic or physiological, the fundamental relations are those of two-valued logic.'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 114) Mental life obeys two-valued logic. Because neurons fire or don't, and the relations among firings respect the rules of propositional logic, mental life as a whole is governed by classical logic. This puts logic at the center of psychology rather than at its periphery.
\par
``Thus both the formal and the final aspects of that activity which we are wont to call mental are rigorously deduceable from present neurophysiology. The psychiatrist may take comfort from the obvious conclusion concerning causality-that, for prognosis, history is never necessary. He can take little from the equally valid conclusion that his observables are explicable only in terms of nervous activities which, until recently, have been beyond his ken. The crux of this ignorance is that inference from any sample of overt behavior to nervous nets is not unique, whereas, of imaginable nets, only one in fact exists, and may, at any moment, exhibit some unpredictable activity. Certainly for the psychiatrist it is more to the point that in such systems ``Mind'' no longer ``goes more ghostly than a ghost.'' Instead, diseased mentality can be understood without loss of scope or rigor, in the scientific terms of neurophysiology. F'' (Mcculloch and Pitts, p. 114) This is a harsh and accurate refutation for Freud, and in general talk therapy techniques.},
  file = {/Users/cillianwhittecar/Zotero/storage/PRUD5L39/Mcculloch and Pitts - A LOGICAL CALCULUS OF THE IDEAS IMMANENT IN NERVOUS ACTIVITY.pdf}
}

