``The justification for this is that the chief reason in favour of any theory on the principles of mathematics must always be inductive, i.e. it must lie in the fact that the theory in question enables us to deduce ordinary mathematics. In mathematics, the greatest degree of self-evidence is usually not to be found quite at the beginning, but at some later point; hence the early deductions, until they reach this point, give reasons rather for believing the premisses because true consequences follow from them, than for believing the consequences because they follow from the premisses.'' (Whitehead and Russell, 2026, p. v)
\par
``All that is affirmed is that the ideas and axioms with which we start are sufficient, not that they are necessary.'' (Whitehead and Russell, 2026, p. vi)
